// [FSMs- Resource Ownership.note]      search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   In order for a SimpleSal App to co-exist with ssTEA and ssUI, mesa resources must be shared.
   The simples configuration is to assert that all resources belong to the App in all states; this
   does not require Apps know how to co-exist and share resources, only how to say politely "mine".
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Resource Ownership implies participants who follow a set of access rules; this is called a MAC.
   If the software accessing the Resource using rules is a MAC, the signal carrier is the variable
   used by the software to track ownership under the rules, the message is the value of each control
   memory used to track ownership. The values and the message of each value are defined by the mesa.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   It is important to recognize that the RO mechanism for software MACs and signal carriers are used
   to communicate ownership, i.e., access, to the MACs providing access to Serial, LED, and Matrix.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The root definition of Arduino co-existence is "sharing the processor to execute all software".
   App events and the App co-exist, which means that given a turn to execute, they do so and return.
   The process of deciding which software (of all the software ready to act when given a turn) is a
   key differentiation between Arduino, ssTEA, an RTOS, or an Operating System such as Linux.  The
   Arduino model supports interrupts but has no mechanism for noninterrupt-based processor sharing.
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   This is a build control for the mesa FSM based on "the shared resource ownership state".
   ssTEA does not have a dependency on the Ascii resource, changes to ssTEA have been made only to
   show that changes to the ssTEA source base is possible, just don't break SimpleSal when you do).
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The "ownership FSM" allows N owners of the resources LED_BUILTIN, the Ascii input/streams, and
   the Matrix. "allows N owners" means "use an FSM to prevent simultaneous access to a resource".
   In the fsmDemo sketch, ownership is held by: 1) the App and friends, or, 2) fsmDemo and friends.
   This is the simplest form, using 2 states: what the scientists call a "binary semaphore".
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   From reset, "the Default owner" has ownership of the resource, until ownership is changed.  When
   the default configuration is put into place, fsmDemo/ssUI/ssTEA adopt the opposite configuration.
   The default configuration is instantiated by loadtime values in the build image, or code running.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   One way to build a version of fsmDemo that is silent is to default Ascii ownership to the App.
   UnoR4 fsmDemo builds in the LED matrix but it is not required for any ssTEA or ssUI functionality.
   One way to build a version of fsmDemo that is dark is to default Matrix ownership to the App.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   typedef enum RO_state_e

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A binary, or 2-state, FSM can be managed using any two values.  The states are mutually exclusive.
   An N-state FSM can have individual states that are mutually exclusive, and similar to binary FSMs.
   In this example, when the binary FSM is in the fsmDemo state, any of the substates may be active
   at the same time, or not.  This requires a bit map to manage which of the substates is active,
   which in turns requires bit map operations to OR bits into and (AND NOT) bits out of the bit map.
   The standard IDE compile settings complain about doing bit-wise operations with enumerated bits.
   The type (int) is the natural language/vocabulary of both the ssHL-language and the processor.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #define mesa_RO_substate_delay       ((int) Bit0)

   This implementation of the RO FSM uses an enumerated set of states and a bit mapping of states.
   For comparison purposes only.  Each is an alternative state-tracking method using data types
   inherent in the processor: integer numbers and bits.  Bits are by definition mutually exclusive
   just as enumerated types are integer numbers that are mutually exclusive.  Testing for a value
   in a set of numbers requires loading a number into a register and comparing other numbers to it.
   Multiple bit-wise comparisons may be made after loading a single bitfield value into a register.
   Enumerated and named values for states helps the human language process understand the meaning.
   The complexity and abstraction of bitfield values can be masked behind parameterized macros.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A compile-time initialization of the variable that will always exist at runtime.  The runtime use
   caused by the first call to "flipstate" makes the FSM believe that the last time through was OFF.
   Another solution is to use uninitialized data, and the random value will be OFF or not OFF: GO!
   Another solution is to push the responsibility to the FSM user and require an initializing OFF.
   All of the solutions depend on a permanent (not on the stack) variable for tracking the state.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   #ifdef SSUI_ONEOF_CONTROL_INIT_RUNTIME

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A flash is defined as "a request, some time OFF, some time ON, then OFF, until requested again".
   This is similar to an electrical digital signal being at the voltage 0, then voltage 1, then 0.
   Transitions in state from 0 to 1 and 1 to 0 are the central signaling mechanism in electronics.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Any chunk of Time is called a "Time period" or just "period".  "60 miles per hour" is a measure
   of the number of miles moved during a one hour period; or one mile per a one minute period; or
   1/60th mile during a one second period.  Those all describe the same movement; different periods.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   void mesa_FSM_LEDflash_Req (void)

